ting that the  increased spread of coping 19  is genuine it's been a really tough time  to be honest and  i think people are from being frank  fed up uh with the situation it's  because they're so complex  um hard to understand and sometimes  there's a conflict with national rules  so for instance the rule of six  uh doesn't apply in the same way to  areas with  local restrictions on gathering in the  homes in homes or in public venues so  it's the confusion that they create and  kind of the the ongoing nature of them  once you're in you never seem to be able  to escape  from them it's like the hotel california  syndrome you know you check in and you  can never leave and i think people are  you know become become quite fed up and  are asking  what difference are they actually making  that's a key question isn't it does feel  as if  what's the exit strategy is there any  way of getting out of these lockdowns  and if you look at the infection rates  in greater manchester  it's pretty depressing they're still  going up why do you think that is  well i go back to when national lockdown  was lifted  i said it then and i say it now it was  lifted too early for the north of  england  the cases were much higher here than  they were in london at the time  and i think it was a decision to lift  lockdown which was mainly made with  london  in mind and we've struggled uh ever  since ever since then  and in terms of what we do from here  i think if we're being fair the  restrictions had an impact initially  in that they dampened the increasing  cases and helped us begin to  bring them back down but i would say  their effectiveness  uh has been limited and diminishes the  longer  that they are in in terms of the exit  strategy well i've got a simple one for  the government  local restrictions should come with  local control  of the test and trace system because  that still  isn't working for greater manchester if  you put that under local control at  least our own destiny would be in our  hands  and we then could work to bring the  levels of  the spread of the virus down in our  boroughs and we'd have an incentive to  be able to get ourselves out of these  restrictions at the moment it feels  that we're a little powerless sophie  we're under these restrictions and we  don't have as much  agencies we need to try and lift them  you were talking about how you think the  restrictions did have a  positive impact driving down case  numbers at the beginning but that's  faded off is that because people are  simply  no longer sticking to the rules what's  your data  on compliance no it's pretty  high i mean they're always going to be  some people who who don't  comply but it it was clearly  the case in the early days that people  did follow the restrictions and as i say  the case numbers came down  what then happened though was a move by  the government working with some of its  mps to  start to create a patchwork of  restrictions so it started to  uh ask where the boroughs should be  withdrawn and then it became very  complicated  uh sophie and then we had uh national  messages which  conflicted with the local restrictions  the rule of six being an example  and more recently the 10 pm curfew that  i've mentioned  so you know all of this becomes really  complicated  so frankly people haven't been  necessarily following the rules  in the way that the government intended  more recently whether or not you blame  the complexity of the rules or the fact  that they didn't get the buy-in or the  government's messaging was wrong  is that the real reason why some of  these local lockdowns have been less  effective  more recently we need a bit of a reset  here  so that people can clearly understand  what's being asked and the government  are talking about tears now aren't they  you know tier one which will be national  restrictions and then a second tier that  would be the additional restrictions on  top of that  that probably would help clarify uh  things  f